# onyx port 0012: UniXNUDxe falls back to starting the application directly
#
# Scope: UniXNUPkg/Driver/UniXNUDxe registers Boot#### for the UniXNU
#   application exactly as before. When that registration cannot be trusted -
#   the application's firmware volume has no device path, or SetVariable fails
#   for Boot####/BootOrder - the driver keeps the volume handle, arms a
#   ReadyToBoot notification and starts the application's PE32 section with
#   LoadImage(TRUE, gImageHandle, NULL, ...) + StartImage exactly once.
#   EndOfDxe is hooked only to log the armed state, because on this platform it
#   is signalled before the platform registers its own default options. Right
#   before starting, ReadyToBoot reads BootOrder back and stands down when the
#   selected option is listed there: BDS signals that event immediately before
#   it would start the option, so a failed BootOrder write must not be read as
#   "BDS will not boot us". Every decision prints a DEBUG_ERROR line prefixed
#   "ONYX-BOOT: ".
# Limits: the fallback is not a second boot manager; it does not run when
#   Boot#### and BootOrder were written, so a working registration boot still
#   proves BDS did the work. It requires the application and this driver to be
#   in the same firmware volume (LoadImage with no device path), it does not
#   change image authentication, and it is unverified on hardware.
# Note: UniXNUPkg is an external UniXNU checkout linked into the Mu-Silicium
#   tree; this patch edits it there, and the DSC/FDF wiring for the driver is
#   already in patch 0009.
# Apply: in the UniXNU checkout itself, not through the Mu tree's mount:
#     ln -s <UniXNU>/package/UniXNUPkg <Mu>/UniXNUPkg   # for the build
#     git -C <UniXNU> apply --directory=package 0012-onyx-unixnudxe-*.patch
#   The paths below are written as UniXNUPkg/... because that is the mount's
#   name, so the core tree needs --directory=package (the checkout holds them
#   under package/UniXNUPkg). Pointing `git -C <Mu> apply` at them does not
#   work: git refuses to write through a symlink that leaves the work tree
#   ("affected file is outside the symlink"), so the mount must be linked after
#   this patch lands rather than used as the apply target.
# Note: this driver compiles against the tree that builds it. Its includes must
#   use Mu-Silicium's MdePkg spelling (<Protocol/FirmwareVolume2.h>), not the
#   legacy <Protocol/FirmwareVolume2Protocol.h>, which that tree does not carry.
diff --git a/UniXNUPkg/Driver/UniXNUDxe/UniXNUDxe.c b/UniXNUPkg/Driver/UniXNUDxe/UniXNUDxe.c
index 213602a..5fceb3c 100644
--- a/UniXNUPkg/Driver/UniXNUDxe/UniXNUDxe.c
+++ b/UniXNUPkg/Driver/UniXNUDxe/UniXNUDxe.c
@@ -6,12 +6,41 @@
   whose variable store is RAM-backed the option is simply re-created on every
   boot.
 
+  On the onyx port BDS never reached the application even though both
+  variables were written, so registration can no longer be the only way in.
+  The driver now falls back to starting the application image directly out of
+  its firmware volume when the registration cannot be trusted:
+
+    - the firmware volume carrying the application has no device path, so no
+      Boot#### can point at it, or
+    - gRT->SetVariable fails for Boot#### or for BootOrder.
+
+  The fallback is armed only in those cases and acts once, on the platform's
+  ReadyToBoot event group, which BDS signals after its own default boot-option
+  registration (MsBootOptionsLibRegisterDefaultBootOptions) and after console
+  connect, but before it gives up with PlatformBootManagerUnableToBoot. When
+  the primary path succeeds nothing is armed, so a working Boot#### boot still
+  proves that BDS started UniXNU. Right before starting, the driver reads
+  BootOrder back and stands down if its own option is listed there, because a
+  failed BootOrder write does not prove BDS will not boot it.
+
+  Limits: the direct start loads the PE32 section of the application file with
+  LoadImage(TRUE, gImageHandle, NULL, ...), which the DXE core accepts because
+  the application and this driver live in the same firmware volume. If that
+  volume is gone, or the file is not an image LoadImage can accept, the
+  fallback reports the EFI_STATUS and BDS keeps its normal failure path. This
+  changes where the application is started from, not whether the image is
+  trusted: image authentication is whatever the platform DXE core already
+  enforces for the FV.
+
